#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230068} :activity/id #uuid "3cf0aacb-2998-5924-ba68-b3d449d0e2b6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050730 :topic/id #uuid "621645f8-2215-52be-860a-5546f675b962" :topic/math-academy-id 6709} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230213 :tutorial/content "In applications, we often build new periodic signals by adding old ones together or scaling their amplitudes.\nThe Fourier series behaves well under these transformations, i.e., scaling and adding functions simply scales and adds their Fourier coefficients.\n\nMore precisely, let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor constants $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ define\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta g(x).\n$$\n\nThen $h$ is also $2\\pi$-periodic, and its Fourier series has the form\n\n$$\nh(x)\\sim \\dfrac{A_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(A_n\\cos(nx)+B_n\\sin(nx)\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the coefficients are obtained by combining the coefficients term-by-term:\n\n- $A_0=\\alpha a_0+\\beta c_0$\n\n- $A_n=\\alpha a_n+\\beta c_n$ for $n\\ge 1$\n\n- $B_n=\\alpha b_n+\\beta d_n$ for $n\\ge 1$\n\nEquivalently,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x)+\\beta g(x)\n\\sim\n\\dfrac{\\alpha a_0+\\beta c_0}{2}\n+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\Big((\\alpha a_n+\\beta c_n)\\cos(nx)+(\\alpha b_n+\\beta d_n)\\sin(nx)\\Big).\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** In this notation, \n\n- the *constant Fourier coefficient* is $A_0,$ while\n\n- the *term* in the series is $\\dfrac{A_0}{2}.$\n\nLet's see a concrete example below." :tutorial/id #uuid "2f038c7e-1fe8-5c76-aec1-ee954aca0b35" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14625 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "284d00c6-9424-5ab8-bc23-10c3e4386c31" :step/math-academy-id 600909 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229173 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228939 :question/id #uuid "cf8f2367-68af-5ef4-ac2e-5a73e37695d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21803"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb295d6c-62fe-59bc-a139-49785ac98f1f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049306}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417682}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity of Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "905bf9c8-d4bc-59ff-a667-9b328a2dd393" :step/math-academy-id 600910 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230219 :tutorial/content "Some simple transformations of a periodic function have very predictable effects on its Fourier coefficients. First, we consider the effect of *adding a constant* (a *vertical shift*).\n\nAssume $f$ has the Fourier series\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nIf $h(x)=f(x)+C$ for a constant $C,$ then only the constant part of the Fourier series changes:\n\n$$\nh(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0+2C}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficients of the transformed series are\n\n$$\na_0^{(h)}=a_0+2C,\n\\qquad\na_n^{(h)}=a_n,\n\\qquad\nb_n^{(h)}=b_n.\n$$\n\nIn the next slides, we will apply this rule to a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "97a65ef1-7e05-5e99-9172-ee34abb62f01" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14645 :tutorial/title "Fourier Series Properties: Vertical Shifting"} :step/id #uuid "8f098fae-dff7-5015-8e23-680da9186bdf" :step/math-academy-id 600913 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230099}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229174 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228941 :question/id #uuid "848cc0af-b940-5ead-81ac-49cc6d2f4989" :question/math-academy-id "e-21805"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f30ca427-3074-56c3-828a-1327d7b172b0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050962}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511576}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Effects of Vertical Shifting and Horizontal Reflection on Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "adb6b87d-19f4-5dca-a59c-1ae6ecde0e28" :step/math-academy-id 600914 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230072}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230218 :tutorial/content "When a $2\\pi$-periodic function has symmetry, many of its Fourier coefficients must be zero. This can simplify Fourier series computations dramatically.\n\nAssume $f(x)$ is $2\\pi$-periodic and piecewise continuous on $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ with Fourier series\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nWe will use the property that the integral of an odd function over a symmetric interval like $[-\\pi, \\pi]$ is always zero.\n\n- If $f$ is an *even* function, meaning $f(-x)=f(x),$ then all sine coefficients $b_n$ must be zero.\nThis is because each $b_n$ is calculated from an integral of an *odd* function:\n\n$$\nb_n = \\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\underbrace{f(x)}_{\\text{even}} \\, \\underbrace{\\sin(nx)}_{\\text{odd}} \\, \\text{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} (\\text{odd function})\\,\\text{d}x = 0.\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of an even function has *cosine terms only*:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos(nx)\n$$\n\n- If $f$ is an *odd* function, meaning $f(-x)=-f(x),$ then the constant and all cosine coefficients (both $a_0$ and $a_n$) must be zero.\nThis is because these coefficients are calculated from integrals of *odd* functions:\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\underbrace{f(x)}_{\\text{odd}} \\, \\underbrace{\\cos(nx)}_{\\text{even}} \\,\\text{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} (\\text{odd function})\\,\\text{d}x = 0 \\quad (\\text{for } n \\ge 0).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of an odd function has *sine terms only*:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}b_n\\sin(nx)\n$$\n\n**Note:** We may use either $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ or $[-\\pi,\\pi]$ as the base interval. Including or excluding the endpoints does not affect the conclusions.\n\nLet's see how this works on concrete examples below." :tutorial/id #uuid "dd9a59ae-0e83-5c4d-9b72-d4db4e1e202a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14644 :tutorial/title "Fourier Series of Odd and Even Functions"} :step/id #uuid "814960c8-4765-557a-b06d-bfea6027ccc2" :step/math-academy-id 600917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230073}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229175 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228940 :question/id #uuid "f43f5028-9314-5123-bbd8-85dc5c4a9b2b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21804"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "532a73e6-e026-57c9-9bad-57cd2f75b6df" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050762}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511555}] :knowledge-point/title "Parity Recognition and Series Type"} :step/id #uuid "a90608fd-d95f-5ba7-bd63-3249845550d6" :step/math-academy-id 600918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230239 :tutorial/content "Another simple transformation is *reflecting a function across the $y$-axis*.\n\nAgain, assume $f$ has the Fourier series\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nIf $h(x)=f(-x),$ then cosine terms stay the same while sine terms change sign (because $\\cos(nx)$ is even and $\\sin(nx)$ is odd):\n\n$$\nh(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)-b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficients of the transformed series are\n\n$$\na_0^{(h)}=a_0,\n\\qquad\na_n^{(h)}=a_n,\n\\qquad\nb_n^{(h)}=-b_n.\n$$\n\nLet's see how to apply this rule in practice on the next slides." :tutorial/id #uuid "71af7376-dcfe-571f-96a9-28dbce65cb76" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14855 :tutorial/title "Fourier Series Properties: Horizontal Reflection"} :step/id #uuid "06e400a8-93bf-593c-8c80-0ecb3f2ca60b" :step/math-academy-id 606272 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230071}}] :activity/title "Properties of Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229265}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511644} :activity/id #uuid "555fcac9-8713-58b2-9317-3fe48ec69606" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050730 :topic/id #uuid "621645f8-2215-52be-860a-5546f675b962" :topic/math-academy-id 6709} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "151c2adc-f498-5355-bc1b-efa89a51b45a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "cosine terms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510827} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4b8479-723c-55f8-8a13-809141b3b771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "sine terms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511011} :answer-field/id #uuid "3130d5c0-7c5e-597f-aea9-6bb1181000b3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b810400-5899-5b7e-9373-4174747a47a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511036} {:answer/id #uuid "b69ee136-7e7e-535f-917e-01a32e6e673d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511159}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511159} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b911936-49df-53cf-9f01-74e0cebc1ad3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8c279eae-dd78-5b67-912d-9a38a48eda28" :question/math-academy-id "q-340800" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the {{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." :question/worked-solution "To check whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-x)) \\\\  & =7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & =7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=f(x),$ we have that $f$ is even.\n\nFor the Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is even and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd, so $f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${b}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{even}$ and the $\\text{sine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero."} :step/id #uuid "59e44bcc-0c2e-57a7-8e71-45cfd8dd1e87" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31897f5a-6e6b-5449-8ab3-590db3d42de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{a}_{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510886} {:answer/id #uuid "919e0113-edd3-5841-879e-86aa411c1a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{b}_{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511081} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7aa80e-855d-54f5-a677-6ef4cbb64763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{5}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511146} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0e1606-796a-518e-aacf-18478af80e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{b}_{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511236} {:answer/id #uuid "fffdf104-47aa-5883-820c-6129aeff7de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511308}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511308} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cd3b6c4-428e-5510-88f8-cbc092e81e64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f0bd5207-abfa-5ab3-9c43-fac18e98df50" :question/math-academy-id "q-340784" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $5$th cosine Fourier coefficient of $f(-x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(-x)$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-{b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $5$th cosine coefficient of $f(-x)$ is ${a}_{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "8c2976be-ba7a-5dd8-a390-f8f1e5aa8929" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e664224-e5b9-5dea-89e9-5258fe549e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{0}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510809} {:answer/id #uuid "3003975d-8590-52c8-854f-7b40173950e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{0}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510884} {:answer/id #uuid "79b9b20a-a095-5016-8fe7-3f0217a39664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{0}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511032} {:answer/id #uuid "cee4def6-0cfa-56ad-99c4-7b9846ee4316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{0}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511211} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e53dcb-9b26-58de-96f2-fc3f376f55dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511224}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511211} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa9d73a9-94dc-5fe0-87cb-3e57fcd56b5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "270a37f5-30dc-586c-8bce-5a858bce0aaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-340776" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the constant Fourier coefficient of  $f(x)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that if $C$ is a constant, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(x)+C$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}+2C$  for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- ${b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nIn our case, $C=4.$ Therefore, the constant Fourier coefficient of  $f(x)+4$ is ${a}_{0}+8.$"} :step/id #uuid "9c211d01-9532-5ebc-88b5-b6935d393a65" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16acd564-8bb4-5f1c-82c3-561b03904c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312131} {:answer/id #uuid "66e3422f-b052-5204-9993-f9b920cdcc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{a}_{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312787} {:answer/id #uuid "ac180436-b1eb-556d-a68d-e61a5de3235e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{b}_{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313323} {:answer/id #uuid "d2744563-6a41-5893-93d0-b2e699ab8ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313621} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d54e34-b41d-5b58-b634-d28765ea5ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{b}_{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313964}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313621} :answer-field/id #uuid "a57dbd7a-67e4-5987-80bd-70ad3b4f1683" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "71f49ef2-3b80-5e3d-890f-74bbd2f89ef7" :question/math-academy-id "q-340782" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $4$th cosine Fourier coefficient of $f(-x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(-x)$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-{b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $4$th cosine coefficient of $f(-x)$ is ${a}_{4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b2b11a51-d093-57da-a2af-6ee6f4f4690b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72a0583c-ea43-573f-b06b-b4a00d3b2c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511020} {:answer/id #uuid "f5668d0f-beb2-5460-abcc-92d140689ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511290} :answer-field/id #uuid "19bcbcdc-3036-501a-8695-55f2eed0f886" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511325} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8459b090-9382-5cc1-a414-5ca52e5a7ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "cosine terms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511053} {:answer/id #uuid "d9aad8a1-e2f8-56c6-bcac-8497da0fc04a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "sine terms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511053} :answer-field/id #uuid "a035b7e1-0b84-5941-bd5f-075b05041e0e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cd373585-64b2-5a1f-ad55-f3dee081e651" :question/math-academy-id "q-340808" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero."} :step/id #uuid "e2232810-d75c-5b33-8c6d-124abfa15358"}] :activity/title "Properties of Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510759}]}
